  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Drift velocity of electrons through a conductor kept in an external electric field&nbsp;is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mi>&#964;</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>&#964;</mi></math>&nbsp;</p><p>Where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo></math>magnitude of charge on electron,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo></math>mass of electron,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo></math>external electric field and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#964;</mi><mo>=</mo></math>average relaxation time.</p><p>As, drift velocity is independent of area. So, the new drift velocity of electrons will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math>.</p>",
